Virtual Memory (Operating System)

Definition:
Virtual memory is a memory management technique that allows the execution of processes even if they are not completely loaded into main memory by using secondary storage as an extension of RAM.

Basic Concept:
Virtual memory creates an illusion of a large memory space by combining main memory and secondary storage. Only the required portions of a program are loaded into RAM while the rest remain on disk.

Working of Virtual Memory:

A process is divided into pages or segments

Required pages are loaded into main memory

If a required page is not in memory, a page fault occurs

The operating system loads the required page from secondary storage

Execution continues after loading the page

Page Fault:
A page fault occurs when a process tries to access a page that is not currently present in main memory.

Advantages of Virtual Memory:

Allows execution of large programs

Improves multiprogramming

Efficient memory utilization

Increases CPU utilization

Provides process isolation

Disadvantages of Virtual Memory:

Page faults cause delay

Increased overhead due to address translation

Thrashing may occur if page faults are frequent

Requires additional hardware support

Demand Paging:
Demand paging is a virtual memory technique where pages are loaded into memory only when they are needed.

Thrashing:
Thrashing is a condition where excessive page faults occur, causing the system to spend more time swapping pages than executing processes.

